## Cardiothoracic surgery

### Post-discharge failure points
Following major cardiac or thoracic procedures, a proportion of patients experience complications in the first 7–21 days at home that are detectable before they become acute. Common failure modes include:

- New or recurrent atrial fibrillation
- Surgical site complications (wound infection, sternal dehiscence)
- Respiratory deterioration (pleural effusion, pneumonia)
- Fluid retention or early heart failure in high-risk patients
- Cardiac medication side effects or adherence issues

Currently, these patients are typically discharged with a 4–6 week outpatient appointment and limited structured contact in between.

### Why this pathway suits structured follow-up
Cardiothoracic patients represent a well-characterised high-risk cohort with known deterioration patterns. The complication profile is identifiable through symptom-based questions. Discharge volumes at major surgical units are sufficient to generate a meaningful evaluation signal.

### What Aescia is designed to surface
- Palpitations, chest discomfort, and irregular heart rhythm symptoms
- Wound appearance changes, heat, discharge, or swelling
- Breathlessness trajectory and functional capacity changes
- Weight gain as a proxy for fluid retention
- Pain that is not tracking toward expected resolution

### Likely escalation destination
Cardiothoracic surgical nursing team, outpatient coordinator, GP with specialist advice, or direct surgical review depending on urgency tier and site protocol.

