## General surgery

### Post-discharge failure points
Post-surgical complications following general procedures (bowel, colorectal, upper GI, hepatobiliary) concentrate in the first 5–14 days. Common failure modes include:

- Surgical site infection
- Anastomotic leak or delayed bowel function recovery
- Deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism risk
- Dehydration and inadequate nutritional intake
- Pain trajectory indicating complications

### Why this pathway suits structured follow-up
General surgical patients are discharged earlier than historical norms due to enhanced recovery pathways. The window between discharge and the first follow-up is wide, and complications in this window frequently present to emergency. Daily structured follow-up fills the gap.

### What Aescia is designed to surface
- Fever, wound redness, or discharge
- Abdominal pain not consistent with expected recovery
- Nausea, vomiting, or inability to tolerate oral intake
- Leg swelling or calf pain
- Deviation from expected functional recovery trajectory

### Likely escalation destination
Surgical nursing review line, GP, outpatient surgical review, or emergency referral depending on urgency tier and site protocol.

