## Heart failure

### Post-discharge failure points
Heart failure carries one of the highest 30-day readmission rates of any inpatient diagnosis. Post-discharge deterioration is often slow and progressive before becoming acute. Common failure modes include:

- Fluid retention and weight gain
- Worsening dyspnoea at reduced exertion thresholds
- Medication non-adherence or side effects
- Dietary non-compliance (sodium, fluid)
- Symptom escalation in the absence of clinical contact

### Why this pathway suits structured follow-up
Daily monitoring of weight and symptoms is already a recommended component of heart failure management; the barrier is systematic delivery and clinical review. Aescia provides the infrastructure to operationalise this recommendation at scale.

### What Aescia is designed to surface
- Daily weight gain above agreed threshold
- Increasing breathlessness with activity or at rest
- Ankle or leg swelling
- Orthopnoea or new nocturnal symptoms
- Fatigue trajectory changes

### Likely escalation destination
Heart failure nurse specialist, cardiologist outpatient review, GP, or hospital admission assessment pathway depending on urgency and site protocol.

