## Respiratory discharge pathways

### Post-discharge failure points
Patients discharged following acute respiratory illness — including community-acquired pneumonia, acute exacerbation of COPD, and asthma — are at risk of deterioration in the first 7–14 days. Common failure modes include:

- Incomplete recovery with symptom relapse
- Inadequate response to antibiotic or steroid therapy
- Functional decline not captured by single endpoint discharge criteria
- Medication adherence and inhaler technique issues

### Why this pathway suits structured follow-up
Respiratory patients represent a large and heterogeneous post-discharge population. Structured follow-up has a strong theoretical basis for early identification of incomplete recovery and escalation before emergency representation.

### What Aescia is designed to surface
- Breathlessness score changes against baseline
- Cough frequency and sputum changes
- Temperature trajectory
- Activity tolerance relative to pre-admission baseline
- Oxygen saturation self-monitoring (where applicable)

### Likely escalation destination
Respiratory nursing, GP, specialist outpatient review, or assessment unit admission depending on urgency tier and site protocol.

